---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Paint Warhammer 40k Miniatures — Beginner's Guide"
  description="A practical painting guide for Warhammer 40k beginners. Learn the three techniques that do 90% of the work, what to actually buy, and how to paint your first model."
>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 border-b border-[#2a2a35]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <p class="text-[#c9a84c] text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-4 font-display">Painting Guide</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">
        It looks harder<br />than it is.
      </h1>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Miniature painting has a reputation for being intimidating. It isn't — 
        at least not at the level you actually need to play the game. 
        Three techniques do 90% of the work. This guide covers all of them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What you need -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">What you actually need</h2>
      <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] mb-8 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Don't buy a full paint set. You'll end up with 30 colors you'll never use and 
        missing the 3 you need constantly. Buy these specifically — 
        the faction guides tell you which colors to add on top.
      </p>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-10">
        <!-- Tools -->
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-lg mb-4">Tools (~$45 one-time)</h3>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            {[
              { item: 'Clippers (sprue cutters)', note: 'Cut models off the sprue. Don\'t use scissors.' },
              { item: 'Hobby knife (X-Acto or similar)', note: 'Trim mold lines off models before painting.' },
              { item: 'Base brush (size 2)', note: 'For basecoating large areas. Any brand.' },
              { item: 'Drybrush (old or cheap)', note: 'A ruined brush works better than a new one.' },
              { item: 'Detail brush (size 0)', note: 'For eyes, gems, and small details.' },
              { item: 'Superglue', note: 'Multi-part plastic kits need this.' },
            ].map(tool => (
              <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
                <span class="text-[#c9a84c] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0">▸</span>
                <div>
                  <span class="text-sm font-semibold">{tool.item}</span>
                  <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] mt-0.5">{tool.note}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Universal paints -->
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-lg mb-4">Universal paints (~$24)</h3>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            {[
              { item: 'Nuln Oil', note: 'Black wash. Works on metal, dark colors, details.' },
              { item: 'Agrax Earthshade', note: 'Brown wash. Works on almost everything.' },
              { item: 'Leadbelcher', note: 'Silver metallic. Every army needs this.' },
              { item: 'Abaddon Black', note: 'True black for weapons and dark areas.' },
              { item: 'Ironbreaker', note: 'Silver for drybrushing metal highlights.' },
            ].map(paint => (
              <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
                <span class="text-[#c9a84c] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0">▸</span>
                <div>
                  <span class="text-sm font-semibold">{paint.item}</span>
                  <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] mt-0.5">{paint.note}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#c9a84c] rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-2">One essential you might miss: spray primer</h3>
        <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Every model needs primer before paint will stick properly. 
          Your faction guide specifies which spray to get — 
          it doubles as your base color so you're not doing extra work. 
          Corax White, Wraithbone, Abaddon Black, Mephiston Red, and others all exist as spray primers. 
          Get the right one for your faction and it saves you an entire step.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Three techniques -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">The three techniques that do 90% of the work</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-10 text-lg">Learn these three things and you can paint any model to a good tabletop standard.</p>

      <!-- Washing -->
      <div class="mb-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">1</div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold">Washing</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-6">
          <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A wash is a thin, dark liquid that flows into recesses and dries there, 
            creating instant shadow and depth. It's the single most powerful technique in miniature painting — 
            one step transforms a flat base coat into something with genuine dimension.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[#b8b4c0]">
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">How to do it:</strong> Load your brush with wash, apply it directly over the painted area. It self-levels into recesses. Don't brush it around — let it flow naturally.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">When to use Nuln Oil:</strong> Metal surfaces, black armor, dark colors, anything mechanical.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">When to use Agrax Earthshade:</strong> Skin, leather, brown/tan colors, fabric, bone — basically everything else.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">The key rule:</strong> Let it dry completely before doing anything else. 15–20 minutes minimum.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Drybrushing -->
      <div class="mb-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">2</div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold">Drybrushing</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-6">
          <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Drybrushing deposits paint only on raised surfaces — edges, ridges, and high points — 
            while leaving recesses dark. It creates highlights and texture without needing any brush control. 
            It's how you make a black model look like dark metal, or make green skin look muscular.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[#b8b4c0]">
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">How to do it:</strong> Load your brush with paint. Wipe almost all of it off on a paper towel until the brush feels almost dry — you should barely see color when you brush your hand. Then brush lightly across the model's raised surfaces. Repeat until you see the highlight building up.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Common mistake:</strong> Too much paint on the brush. The brush should feel practically empty. If paint is going on in thick streaks, you have too much — wipe more off.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Use old brushes:</strong> Drybrushing destroys brush tips. Use a designated drybrush or an old one you don't care about.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Contrast / Layer -->
      <div class="mb-4">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">3</div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold">Contrast Paints</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-6">
          <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Contrast paints are the single biggest improvement in beginner painting in the last decade. 
            Applied over a light base coat (Wraithbone or Grey Seer spray), 
            they flow into recesses like a wash while leaving raised areas bright — 
            combining base coat and shade in one step. 
            One coat of the right Contrast paint can finish an entire section of a model.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[#b8b4c0]">
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">The catch:</strong> They only work over light primers (Wraithbone, Grey Seer, Corax White). Over dark primer, they look muddy and wrong.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">How to apply:</strong> Load brush, apply generously. Don't overwork it — one pass and leave it. Let it flow where it wants to go.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Best uses:</strong> Skin, cloth, leather, fur, organic surfaces. Less effective on flat armor plates where you want an even color.</p>
            <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Every faction guide specifies which Contrast paints to use.</strong> They're not required, but they make life significantly easier.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- The process -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">Your first model — the process</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-10 text-lg">Every model, every faction, every skill level follows the same sequence. This is the order. Don't skip steps.</p>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        {[
          {
            step: 'Clean and assemble',
            detail: 'Remove mold lines with your hobby knife — thin raised lines running across the model from the casting process. Then clip parts off the sprue and glue. Let glue cure before moving on.',
            warn: null,
          },
          {
            step: 'Prime',
            detail: 'Spray primer from about 30cm (12 inches) away in short, sweeping passes. Rotate the model to get all angles. Thin coats. The goal is full coverage without obscuring detail.',
            warn: 'Don\'t prime in humid weather or rain — the paint will bubble.',
          },
          {
            step: 'Basecoat the main color',
            detail: 'If your primer is already the right color (most are), this step is done. If you need a different color on parts of the model, apply it now with a base brush. Use thin coats — two thin coats cover better than one thick one.',
            warn: null,
          },
          {
            step: 'Block in secondary colors',
            detail: 'Apply your other colors — leather, skin, metal areas. Don\'t worry about being perfect. Small overlaps get fixed in later steps. Basecoating is about coverage, not precision.',
            warn: null,
          },
          {
            step: 'Wash everything',
            detail: 'Apply your wash over the whole model or specific sections. Agrax over organic areas, Nuln Oil over metal. Let it flow into recesses. Walk away and let it dry completely.',
            warn: 'Patience here is everything. Applying anything over a wet wash ruins it.',
          },
          {
            step: 'Drybrush/highlight raised surfaces',
            detail: 'Drybrush a lighter version of your main color over raised armor edges, muscles, fur, or texture. This brings the model back to life after the wash darkened everything.',
            warn: null,
          },
          {
            step: 'Paint the details',
            detail: 'Eyes, gems, lenses, teeth, weapon markings. Small details with your detail brush. These take the most time per square millimeter but have the biggest visual impact.',
            warn: null,
          },
          {
            step: 'Base the model',
            detail: 'Apply texture paint to the base (not the model\'s feet). Let it dry. Drybrush a lighter color over the texture. Add a tuft or two. Done.',
            warn: null,
          },
        ].map((s, i) => (
          <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <div class="w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">{i + 1}</div>
              <div class="flex-1">
                <p class="font-semibold mb-1">{s.step}</p>
                <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm leading-relaxed">{s.detail}</p>
                {s.warn && (
                  <p class="text-amber-500 text-xs mt-2 font-semibold">⚠ {s.warn}</p>
                )}
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Common mistakes -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-8">Common mistakes — and how to avoid them</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        {[
          {
            mistake: 'Too much paint on the brush',
            fix: 'Wipe the side of the brush on the pot rim before applying. The brush should be loaded but not dripping. Excess paint causes clumping and obscures detail.',
          },
          {
            mistake: 'Paint too thick (gloopy, grainy texture)',
            fix: 'Add a tiny drop of water to your paint and mix. The consistency should be like skimmed milk — thin enough to flow but not so thin it loses color. Thin coats over two passes beat one thick coat every time.',
          },
          {
            mistake: 'Painting in bad light',
            fix: 'Get a daylight lamp. The difference is massive — you\'ll see detail you couldn\'t see before, and your colors will look more accurate. A cheap desk lamp with a daylight bulb works.',
          },
          {
            mistake: 'Skipping primer',
            fix: 'Paint applied directly to plastic doesn\'t stick properly and chips at the slightest touch. Always prime. Always.',
          },
          {
            mistake: 'Rushing between steps',
            fix: 'Wet wash + wet layer paint = streaky disaster. If you\'re not sure if something is dry, wait another five minutes. You cannot rush drying.',
          },
          {
            mistake: 'Starting on your best model',
            fix: 'Practice on a Poxwalker, a Guardsman, or a Gretchin — cheap, small, forgiving. Save the hero model for when you know what you\'re doing.',
          },
          {
            mistake: 'Thinking "tabletop standard" isn\'t good enough',
            fix: 'At arm\'s length on a table, a basecoat + wash + drybrush model looks great. The goal is a painted army on the table, not a display cabinet piece. One well-painted squad beats a pile of grey plastic every time.',
          },
        ].map(item => (
          <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
            <p class="text-red-400 text-sm font-semibold mb-1">✗ {item.mistake}</p>
            <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed">{item.fix}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Batch painting -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">Batch painting — how to actually finish an army</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg mb-8">
        <p>
          Painting one model at a time from start to finish is inefficient and demoralizing — 
          you'll burn out long before you have an army. 
          The solution is batch painting: doing one step across all your models before moving to the next.
        </p>
        <p>
          The method: line up 10 models (or 5 if they're complex). 
          Do step 1 on all 10. By the time you've done the tenth, the first has dried and you can move to step 2. 
          You're effectively painting in parallel. 
          A squad that might take 3 hours painted individually takes 90 minutes in a batch of 10.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3">The batch size rule</h3>
        <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[#b8b4c0]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Simple models (Poxwalkers, Gretchin, Guardsmen):</strong> Batch 10–20 at a time.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Medium models (Space Marines, Necron Warriors):</strong> Batch 5–10.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Complex models (characters, monsters):</strong> Batch 1–3. These get individual attention.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Vehicles:</strong> One at a time — too big to benefit from batching, too complex to rush.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Going further -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">When you want to go further</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-8 text-lg">Once you're comfortable with the basics, these techniques are the next step up.</p>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        {[
          {
            name: 'Edge highlighting',
            desc: 'A thin, precise line of lighter color along the sharpest armor edges. Requires a steady hand and a fine brush. The technique that makes Aeldari look spectacular.',
            difficulty: 'Intermediate',
          },
          {
            name: 'Layering',
            desc: 'Building up multiple thin layers of progressively lighter color on raised surfaces — a smoother, more realistic highlight than drybrushing. Takes more time but looks cleaner.',
            difficulty: 'Intermediate',
          },
          {
            name: 'Wet blending',
            desc: 'Mixing two colors while they\'re still wet on the model to create a seamless gradient. Used for cloaks, skin, and dramatic color transitions.',
            difficulty: 'Advanced',
          },
          {
            name: 'OSL (Object Source Lighting)',
            desc: 'Painting the glow of a light source (a weapon, a gem, an eye) radiating outward onto the surrounding model. Creates dramatic, cinematic effects.',
            difficulty: 'Advanced',
          },
          {
            name: 'Sponge chipping',
            desc: 'Torn foam sponge dipped lightly in metallic paint, dabbed onto armor edges for instant battle damage. Easy, fast, and looks great on Chaos and Imperial models.',
            difficulty: 'Easy',
          },
          {
            name: 'Technical paints',
            desc: 'Typhus Corrosion for chunky rust, Nurgle\'s Rot for slime, Blood for the Blood God for wet gore. Each creates a specific effect that would be very hard to fake otherwise.',
            difficulty: 'Easy',
          },
        ].map(tech => (
          <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="font-display font-semibold">{tech.name}</h3>
              <span class={`text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded font-display flex-shrink-0 ml-2 ${
                tech.difficulty === 'Easy' ? 'bg-green-900 text-green-300' :
                tech.difficulty === 'Intermediate' ? 'bg-yellow-900 text-yellow-300' :
                'bg-red-900 text-red-300'
              }`}>{tech.difficulty}</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm leading-relaxed">{tech.desc}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="px-6 py-20 text-center">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-4">The first model is the hardest.</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg mb-8">
        After that, each one gets faster and better. 
        Don't wait until you feel ready — you'll never feel ready. 
        Start with one model, use the steps above, and see what you make.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
        <a
          href="/factions"
          class="inline-block bg-[#c9a84c] hover:bg-[#dfc070] text-[#111113] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display tracking-wide"
        >
          Find Your Faction →
        </a>
        <a
          href="/quiz"
          class="inline-block border border-[#2a2a35] hover:border-[#c9a84c] text-[#b8b4c0] hover:text-[#c9a84c] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display"
        >
          Take the Army Quiz
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
